00:38.040 --> 00:40.780
All right, hello and welcome to Camel Finance. I'm your boy Camel.

00:40.940 --> 00:46.440
And today I'm going to talk a little bit about long-term versus short-term trading strategies on this channel.

00:46.520 --> 00:55.460
I had a number of questions around these areas and I said something in yesterday's video along the lines of I might start to DCA into some of the minor positions,

00:55.460 --> 01:00.040
even if I buy in the long-term account and then the price moves lower, I'll start to add more.

01:00.300 --> 01:04.860
And a lot of people were like, whoa, whoa, you can't do that. Losers average losers, which you're right, half right.

01:05.220 --> 01:08.740
And people were like, I don't think we do that. Stop is a stop for a reason. It's invalidation.

01:08.800 --> 01:10.560
And yes, you're all correct. You're absolutely correct.

01:10.680 --> 01:16.080
But I have two separate systems and strategies, one for short-term trading and one for long-term trading.

01:16.080 --> 01:19.660
So I wanted to clarify that today. I think this should add quite a bit of value, hopefully.

01:19.660 --> 01:25.380
We did indeed get the hike. Okay. So, you know, no surprises on this channel. I didn't expect us to hike.

01:25.500 --> 01:29.260
But again, tell that to the bond market, right? That's the whole point of this thing.

01:29.360 --> 01:31.640
It doesn't matter. Pause your screen and read that if you're interested.

01:31.960 --> 01:36.760
It doesn't matter whether or not I think we're going to pause or I think we shouldn't hike or any of that.

01:36.840 --> 01:40.800
What matters is what the bond market price is in. And the bond market indeed priced in a rate hike.

01:40.900 --> 01:44.080
That's how we knew we were probably going to get a rate hike. And indeed, that's what we've seen.

01:44.080 --> 01:50.700
So as it stands, about 50-50 going into October's meeting. Again, it is my opinion that hopefully that was one and done.

01:51.180 --> 01:57.280
And I think that would set up all the cycles and the trade setups I've been talking about for months now on this channel.

01:57.560 --> 02:00.120
But again, it doesn't matter what I think. It doesn't matter what my opinion is.

02:00.160 --> 02:03.960
If this thing goes in this direction, then we'll see another hike. Okay. Tell back to the bond market.

02:04.200 --> 02:08.740
That's the first bit of alpha, right? Everyone concerns themselves. Everyone likes to bet. Everyone likes to gamble and speculate.

02:08.740 --> 02:12.880
But the reality is you just have to look at the bond futures, which is what this tool is derived from.

02:12.880 --> 02:17.920
And the bond market in aggregate is what sets rates. So that's why we say tell that to the bond market.

02:18.080 --> 02:22.880
I didn't listen to FOMC. I'm not really interested in the rhetoric or whether it was dovish or hawkish or whatever.

02:22.880 --> 02:29.580
Because I don't really believe any of it means anything. I think they're all just kind of on stage to read their scripts and jerk the tape slaves around.

02:29.740 --> 02:35.480
So that's all I have to say about that. Other than, I don't know if people realize just how good we had it.

02:36.680 --> 02:37.320
Good afternoon.

02:40.640 --> 02:41.700
Alright, that's enough of that.

02:42.880 --> 02:48.160
I was talking about DCA-ing down into some of these minor positions in the long-term account.

02:48.320 --> 02:50.720
And it sort of set off a bunch of people's spider senses.

02:50.860 --> 02:54.080
And that was correct. And I'm glad and I'm pleased people understand this.

02:54.160 --> 02:56.700
But do not confuse the long-term and the short-term account.

02:56.840 --> 03:05.420
Okay. So I think the easiest way for me to explain this is to first talk about how this year has gone in terms of the short-term account for trading the stock market.

03:05.420 --> 03:11.100
People will remember we were short from here. Okay. Just in case we were going to start the bear market decline into the four-year low.

03:11.200 --> 03:14.940
At the bottom, sentiment didn't match. And I flipped long in the Dow Jones.

03:15.260 --> 03:20.040
We caught this move up, added a position here, and that position got stopped out on this wick.

03:20.300 --> 03:23.200
At the time that was stopped out, I said, okay, just give me my money.

03:23.200 --> 03:26.840
I'm going to lock in the gains from the Dow Jones trade and wait for the upcoming daily cycle low.

03:27.140 --> 03:28.920
And so there's a few key points here. Okay.

03:28.920 --> 03:32.260
When we're talking about the short-term account, we're talking about, for me, derivatives.

03:32.580 --> 03:34.300
Okay. This is leveraged by nature.

03:34.580 --> 03:38.060
We are talking about a hard and fast 2% risk per trade rule.

03:38.160 --> 03:43.500
And I do not change that 2% up and down depending on conviction or market conditions or anything else.

03:43.500 --> 03:45.360
That is a fixed and rigid number.

03:45.780 --> 03:51.480
The reason for this is, let's say, I bet 2% per trade and I get stopped out three times in a row.

03:51.640 --> 03:54.140
Well, needless to say, I will lose 6% of my account.

03:54.340 --> 03:59.260
If I then allow my mind to do what it loves to do, which is convince me how not to trade.

03:59.400 --> 04:01.720
Okay. I might say, well, I better slow this down a bit.

04:01.800 --> 04:05.320
I better go 1% risk per trade until I start winning again.

04:05.320 --> 04:10.860
But if I then go 1% on the fourth trade and it's a banger trade and we hit 5R, well, I make 5%.

04:10.860 --> 04:14.860
So in that scenario, I've lost 2 and 2 and 2, which of course is 6.

04:15.140 --> 04:18.500
And then when I finally hit a good 5R trade, I only had half my exposure

04:18.500 --> 04:24.140
because I allowed myself to talk myself into sizing down to 1% to, quote, manage risk further.

04:24.340 --> 04:30.000
But in doing so, after hitting a 5R trade, I'm still down 1% because I lost 6% on the first three.

04:30.340 --> 04:33.120
Then I halved my position size and had 1% risk per trade.

04:33.120 --> 04:35.020
If that moves 5R, I make 5%.

04:35.020 --> 04:36.780
So I'm still in the hole for 1%.

04:36.780 --> 04:40.240
First is, if I keep my risk fixed at 2% per trade,

04:40.240 --> 04:43.100
then I lose the 6% on the three stops in a row.

04:43.380 --> 04:47.380
And on the fourth one, hitting 5R puts me at 10% account gain.

04:47.660 --> 04:50.380
And thus, even though I've only been right one out of four trades,

04:50.540 --> 04:52.280
I'm still up 4% on the account.

04:52.420 --> 04:53.320
That's really important.

04:53.640 --> 04:55.560
Keep that risk per trade fixed.

04:55.700 --> 04:57.280
Okay. If you start shoving it up and down,

04:57.360 --> 05:00.980
you're just going to find yourself paying more stops at a higher level.

05:00.980 --> 05:04.280
And then when your trades work out, you're going to be underexposed,

05:04.400 --> 05:06.600
not be able to recover the stop losses and the gains.

05:06.740 --> 05:08.300
I always use hard stops as well.

05:08.300 --> 05:10.340
That's another big thing for the short-term account.

05:10.500 --> 05:13.440
Because it's leveraged, because I need sniper precise entries,

05:13.500 --> 05:15.320
because overnight fees and stuff are expensive,

05:15.320 --> 05:18.680
I need to have an area on the chart where if that level is run, I'm out of the trade.

05:18.780 --> 05:20.580
Okay. I'm wrong. And I move on to the next one.

05:20.620 --> 05:22.380
I need to have that because on leverage,

05:22.440 --> 05:25.540
that 2% can very quickly turn into an unacceptably large number.

05:25.540 --> 05:28.860
And then similarly, and this is where a lot of people's spidey senses were set off.

05:28.940 --> 05:32.520
There is the old Mark Minovini saying, which is losers, average losers.

05:32.780 --> 05:37.060
Meaning you shouldn't DCA down into a position or add to a losing position.

05:37.060 --> 05:41.740
And instead, you should look to buy a market that continues to push you into profit,

05:41.880 --> 05:44.240
which is the complete opposite of how most people trade, right?

05:44.260 --> 05:48.860
Most people, they like a stock at X, it drops 20%, and they like it even more, right?

05:48.900 --> 05:52.580
But that kind of generally doesn't work when you're talking about short-term trading, okay?

05:52.600 --> 05:54.260
If I'm stopped out and I see failed cycles,

05:54.320 --> 05:57.660
the last thing I want to do is be dogmatic about it and start saying,

05:57.760 --> 05:59.240
well, I'm going to be right eventually.

05:59.360 --> 06:01.500
Let me just continue to add on the way down.

06:01.560 --> 06:05.720
Because very quickly then, you're backing a loser or a downtrend, okay?

06:05.720 --> 06:07.880
So in the short-term account, we definitely don't want to do that.

06:08.160 --> 06:10.520
So going back here with a real example, okay?

06:10.540 --> 06:12.940
We short this, we catch the gain, get long the Dow.

06:13.140 --> 06:15.460
It moves into something like 3R, okay?

06:15.520 --> 06:17.080
And then we give back 1R.

06:17.340 --> 06:19.740
So in other words, it moves 6% into profit.

06:20.060 --> 06:23.360
We give back 1%, 2% trade in here once it fails.

06:23.700 --> 06:24.940
And I said to the members at the time,

06:25.000 --> 06:28.440
look, I'm just going to cut the Dow and lock in the gain so I can still walk away net green.

06:28.700 --> 06:30.100
We then proceeded to wait until here,

06:30.320 --> 06:32.900
where I bought another 2% account risk allocation,

06:33.100 --> 06:34.940
this time spreading it across three assets.

06:34.940 --> 06:37.600
So I bought the US 100, the Dow Jones and the Russell.

06:38.040 --> 06:41.180
So far, the Russell and the Dow have both been stopped out.

06:41.460 --> 06:44.860
And I think, painfully so, that's probably pretty much the bottom.

06:45.260 --> 06:46.000
But that's okay.

06:46.300 --> 06:49.260
And then the US 100 has still got some profit in here.

06:49.480 --> 06:51.060
So notice what's happened, okay?

06:51.100 --> 06:51.880
Going back to here.

06:52.100 --> 06:55.320
For the start of the year, catch the downside here.

06:55.620 --> 06:57.500
Account is green from the stock market perspective.

06:57.760 --> 06:58.700
Catch a good move here.

06:58.980 --> 07:01.400
Give some of it back, but then lock in the gains.

07:01.640 --> 07:02.380
Still net green.

07:02.380 --> 07:03.280
Wait till here.

07:03.640 --> 07:04.700
High probability set up.

07:04.880 --> 07:07.780
Easy, clean area on the chart to manage my risk around.

07:08.100 --> 07:08.900
Add some exposure.

07:09.280 --> 07:12.840
Some of it gets given back, but I still get to keep some in the form of the NASDAQ.

07:12.980 --> 07:13.220
Okay?

07:13.360 --> 07:15.820
And now we're imminently due another daily cycle low,

07:15.900 --> 07:17.880
where I can add back the exposure that was stopped.

07:18.080 --> 07:19.660
And so long as we get another push from here,

07:19.940 --> 07:23.840
still net green on the stock market portion, despite paying a bunch of stops.

07:24.000 --> 07:26.680
But the reason this works is because, like I said earlier,

07:26.680 --> 07:29.040
we keep the risk per trade fixed.

07:29.360 --> 07:34.100
We have hard stops so that if the cycle fails or the trade invalidates, we're out.

07:34.300 --> 07:36.240
And then we just simply sit back and trade the edge,

07:36.260 --> 07:39.860
which is to say we wait for the next high probability daily cycle low to add exposure around.

07:40.080 --> 07:42.400
And so long as this daily cycle low resolves to more upside,

07:42.500 --> 07:43.900
which I'm pretty sure it will,

07:44.260 --> 07:48.480
then again, we'll be able to squeeze more than 2% gain out of the market.

07:48.720 --> 07:50.860
And thus, this whole move for this year in the stocks,

07:50.960 --> 07:53.780
although we would have paid a few stops, will still result to profit.

07:53.780 --> 07:54.060
Okay?

07:54.180 --> 07:55.180
That's all trading is.

07:55.220 --> 07:56.900
It's like two steps forward, one step back.

07:57.020 --> 08:00.040
But the only way you can make sure that you're going two steps forward,

08:00.400 --> 08:04.880
one step back, instead of two steps forward and then two or three steps back,

08:04.940 --> 08:07.080
is you have to do the aforementioned, okay?

08:07.140 --> 08:09.980
Standard trade size, hard stop losses, not averaging down.

08:10.240 --> 08:14.100
And sticking to the edge, not just buying a daily cycle low here before it confirms

08:14.100 --> 08:15.980
or doing anything silly like that.

08:16.120 --> 08:17.760
Then when we get to the longer term account,

08:18.020 --> 08:21.200
instead of have a 2% account risk per trade,

08:21.200 --> 08:24.540
as per the risk management and leverage tutorial in the playlist,

08:24.880 --> 08:29.120
I manage my risk by absolute position sizing and assuming it can go to zero.

08:29.380 --> 08:33.440
So that means if I put a 5% trade on here, 5% of my long-term account,

08:33.520 --> 08:36.020
and let's be clear, this is just buying shares, right?

08:36.080 --> 08:37.780
In a SIP or an ISER or whatever.

08:37.900 --> 08:39.400
They call this a spot allocation.

08:39.540 --> 08:40.720
I know some of you like that term.

08:40.960 --> 08:42.300
So I allocate 5% here.

08:42.480 --> 08:45.480
If it drops 50%, then I'm going to lose 2.5%.

08:45.480 --> 08:46.920
And of course, some of you will be thinking,

08:47.040 --> 08:48.860
well, hang on, 2.5% is bigger than 2%.

08:48.860 --> 08:49.140
Yes.

08:49.140 --> 08:50.800
But like I said, it's a different strategy.

08:51.200 --> 08:55.520
In the long-term account, I'm more than willing to tolerate 6%, even 10% drawdown,

08:55.780 --> 09:00.020
because that's the upper bound for where the math starts to compound against you.

09:00.120 --> 09:04.660
If you lose 10% of your account, it takes an 11% following trade to get back to breakeven.

09:04.960 --> 09:08.860
After that, you start to need to hit massive trades just to get back to breakeven.

09:09.020 --> 09:10.540
So it starts to compound against you.

09:10.820 --> 09:14.940
And if you really think about it, 6% is also the sector risk rule in the short-term account.

09:14.940 --> 09:19.320
So it's pretty common for me to have 6% risk once I've earned the right to have it.

09:19.460 --> 09:21.360
But like I said, when we get to the long-term account,

09:21.420 --> 09:23.640
we're talking about allocating 5% here, let's say.

09:23.840 --> 09:26.320
If it goes all the way to zero, I'll lose 5% of the account.

09:26.580 --> 09:27.920
Again, risk is still pretty managed.

09:28.120 --> 09:31.240
I only need the next trade to move up 6% and I'm back in profit.

09:31.440 --> 09:33.880
Unlike if it was to lose 50% of my account,

09:33.940 --> 09:38.260
the next trade would need to be a 100% upside move just to get back to breakeven.

09:38.480 --> 09:40.700
Again, that's the importance of having the correct sizing.

09:41.020 --> 09:42.680
So then we add here, we get all of this.

09:42.820 --> 09:46.400
Okay, by the time we get here, I've got enough gains from the first trade to say,

09:46.500 --> 09:48.140
okay, I can add more exposure here.

09:48.240 --> 09:49.340
Let's add another 5%.

09:49.340 --> 09:52.860
And then make sure that if this cycle were to fail, we cut the entire position.

09:53.120 --> 09:55.960
Meaning I lock in the gains from the first trade from here to here.

09:56.020 --> 09:58.620
And then I give back a little bit from here to here.

09:58.840 --> 10:00.080
And then we just repeat this process.

10:00.180 --> 10:00.960
Okay, we move up here.

10:01.020 --> 10:02.960
I say, okay, time to add a bit more in the longer-term account.

10:02.960 --> 10:08.840
Worst case scenario, this cycle here fails and I give back the gains from here to here on this trade.

10:08.840 --> 10:12.100
But we lock in here to here and we lock in here to here.

10:12.380 --> 10:13.580
And then it's the same deal up here.

10:13.660 --> 10:15.640
Okay, we add again in the long-term account.

10:15.800 --> 10:19.740
Once we get this daily cycle load in and confirmed, I will draw a line underneath that.

10:19.920 --> 10:25.740
And if that cycle fails, then I will obviously give back the gains from about here to here on the most recent add.

10:25.920 --> 10:30.260
But I will get to lock in the gains from here to here and from here to here and from here to here.

10:30.260 --> 10:35.640
So you can see there's quite a different strategy in terms of the long-term versus short-term account.

10:35.980 --> 10:43.300
Also, I don't necessarily need to run hard stops in here because if I allocate 5%, like I said, it's got to go all the way down 50%.

10:43.300 --> 10:44.860
And then I can just manually cut the trade.

10:44.920 --> 10:49.520
And I'd like to think I'm not going to watch this position move down 50% before I think about stepping in and cutting it.

10:49.600 --> 10:51.660
You can, of course, use hard stops if you want to.

10:51.800 --> 10:58.200
I personally find in the UK a lot of accounts for longer-term ideas like SIPs or ICES or whatever.

10:58.200 --> 11:01.100
I generally find the stop losses are not very good.

11:01.340 --> 11:02.320
They're not very reliable.

11:02.680 --> 11:05.380
They often don't feel at the level they're supposed to feel at.

11:05.640 --> 11:11.160
Many of them will only let you set it for a fixed period of time, like 30 or 60 or 90 days at a time.

11:11.220 --> 11:15.660
And then you have to remember to come back and add that stop back in or that sell limit.

11:15.880 --> 11:17.200
For me, I just don't have the time.

11:17.280 --> 11:18.420
I don't have the patience for it.

11:18.480 --> 11:20.280
So I just manage by absolute sizing.

11:20.480 --> 11:21.860
Again, do we average losers?

11:21.960 --> 11:23.480
No, you generally shouldn't average losers.

11:23.480 --> 11:28.240
But sometimes you can look at the cycles and say, okay, I think this is the yearly cycle low here.

11:28.480 --> 11:31.280
I know it's got a plus or minus one weekly cycle of tolerance.

11:31.620 --> 11:34.580
So anywhere inside of this window will do.

11:34.720 --> 11:36.900
That will be near enough the lows to call it the lows.

11:37.180 --> 11:40.420
And if you think about it, I've already just demonstrated this in real time with TLT.

11:40.800 --> 11:45.640
So in here, we started to say, okay, we're getting to the neighborhood where we're going to be approaching a seven and a half year cycle low.

11:45.780 --> 11:51.200
Once we got about a weekly cycle away from the seven and a half year cycle, I started to add some exposure.

11:51.200 --> 11:53.700
I added 10% initially of the long-term account.

11:53.780 --> 11:58.800
And remember, I might sound like a lot, but that would have to go all the way to zero for me to lose that 10%.

11:58.800 --> 12:02.560
Okay, if it goes down 50%, I'll lose 5% and so on and so forth.

12:02.700 --> 12:06.840
Then when we got to the next weekly cycle, I started to say, okay, I think we can average in here again.

12:06.940 --> 12:08.640
Okay, and I've got 20% account exposure.

12:08.760 --> 12:09.420
This is very public.

12:09.540 --> 12:11.820
It's all tracked and traced in real time in the level three member section.

12:12.100 --> 12:15.220
And then we were talking about what if we get a sweep of the lows, which is quite probable,

12:15.480 --> 12:18.480
followed by what should be a turnaround and then a push to the upside.

12:18.480 --> 12:22.600
I may even add more once we get that confirmed bottom in.

12:22.840 --> 12:27.280
But as I've shown several times, I had a couple of stabs at this, okay, and it's in the long-term account.

12:27.360 --> 12:27.980
The risk is managed.

12:28.060 --> 12:30.880
My average entry is about 84, might be 85.

12:31.160 --> 12:31.560
I don't know.

12:31.620 --> 12:34.520
It'll be in the post tab of the member section, but let's just call it 85.

12:34.920 --> 12:39.700
If this was to fall all the way down here to 76, that would be 10% of 20%.

12:39.700 --> 12:42.700
Only 2% of my actual long-term account is at risk.

12:42.700 --> 12:47.140
If it was to fall 20%, of course, which would be all the way down here, I'd have to be thinking,

12:47.340 --> 12:48.820
okay, I'm fully wrong on this idea.

12:49.100 --> 12:49.900
Time to cut the trade.

12:49.960 --> 12:51.360
And I would lose 4% of the account.

12:51.660 --> 12:54.400
So again, risk is managed despite having large exposure.

12:54.520 --> 12:59.840
Now, would I put 20% into something as volatile as MicroStrategy in one go or in two clips?

12:59.900 --> 13:00.420
Probably not.

13:00.680 --> 13:03.760
MicroStrategy is far more likely to have a 20% drawdown than TLT is.

13:03.860 --> 13:06.540
So you have to take into account the asset volatility.

13:07.020 --> 13:09.920
But I mean, if it does this, then we're clearly wrong on this idea.

13:10.240 --> 13:12.520
Time to cut the trade long before 20%, I would think.

13:12.520 --> 13:15.660
And it's not going to leave me needing to fight my way out of a hole, okay?

13:15.680 --> 13:18.220
I can recover 4%, no problem at all.

13:18.320 --> 13:23.140
From 85, 4% up here, that puts us at what, like 88, okay?

13:23.480 --> 13:27.380
Shouldn't be that difficult to find a trade that can move up that much for the next allocation.

13:27.700 --> 13:29.040
So I hope that clears that up.

13:29.160 --> 13:31.520
Generally speaking, I don't really average into positions.

13:31.760 --> 13:36.700
But with that said, there are times when we're in the window now for gold, for example, right?

13:36.700 --> 13:40.400
We could have the weekly cycle in here, and that could be the four-year cycle load too,

13:40.400 --> 13:42.700
in which case we'll probably see a higher low and start to go.

13:42.880 --> 13:46.700
So I'll just be looking to allocate around the upcoming confirmed low once it's in and confirmed.

13:46.920 --> 13:50.600
It could be that we're going to come down here and do this, in which case, same deal.

13:51.000 --> 13:54.260
And in the worst case scenario, this could be the weekly cycle left translated,

13:54.500 --> 13:56.820
and now we're heading down to a low kind of in March time.

13:56.920 --> 14:00.520
In which case, if I buy here and it does this, and then I wait and buy here,

14:00.600 --> 14:03.340
I get an average price around here in the long-term account.

14:03.340 --> 14:08.460
And as somebody that is extremely bullish, gold, miners, silver, et al, over the rest

14:08.460 --> 14:13.340
of the decade and even into around the early 30s, I'm okay with this, okay?

14:13.400 --> 14:14.960
I can start to nibble exposure here.

14:15.020 --> 14:18.680
If it starts to blast higher, then we'll start to more aggressively increase exposure.

14:18.840 --> 14:22.720
If we start to see this and then it moves down, okay, I might nibble a bit more on the

14:22.720 --> 14:27.580
next daily or weekly cycle and repeat this process until we are at the outside tolerance

14:27.580 --> 14:28.680
